const FOUNDATION_GLOSSARY: NewPlayerKeyword[] = [
  { term: "BO1 / BO3", explanation: "BO1 is best of one: a single game decides the match. BO3 is best of three: the first player to win two games wins the match. This course teaches BO3 setup.", example: "Reach eight points to win one game. Winning a second game wins a BO3 match; points do not carry over between games." },
  { term: "Domain / identity", explanation: "A domain is a card family, shown by a symbol. Your legend’s domains form your identity: the families your deck may contain.", example: "Ahri’s Calm/Mind identity allows Calm and Mind cards. Her Mind power cost still needs Mind power, even if you have spare Calm power." },
  { term: "Channel", explanation: "Bring runes from your rune deck onto the table. A rune is not spendable energy just because it arrived; use its ability to make the resource you need.", example: "Channel two ready runes, then turn each sideways to make two energy for a Sentry." },
  { term: "Showdown / focus", explanation: "A showdown is a chance to play around a contested battlefield before the contest settles. Focus determines who may start the next action there while the chain is empty. Passing offers that chance to the other player.", example: "After a unit moves onto a contested battlefield, players can use permitted cards before combat. If both pass without adding anything, the showdown can finish." },
  { term: "Resolve", explanation: "Carry out a waiting spell or ability’s instructions. Paying its cost and announcing it happen earlier, giving players a chance to respond first.", example: "Discipline only adds Might when it resolves. Putting it on the chain does not raise the unit’s Might yet." },
  { term: "Target", explanation: "The specific card or other object an effect asks you to choose. It must meet the restrictions written on that effect.", example: "Defy targets a qualifying spell waiting on the chain. Clicking the unit that spell would affect does not choose the spell." },
  { term: "Action", explanation: "Adds permission to play a card or activate its ability during an open showdown. It does not, by itself, allow a response to an existing chain.", example: "An Action can be used in the combat showdown after the chain is empty and you have the appropriate permission." },
  { term: "Reaction", explanation: "Includes Action timing and can also respond while a chain exists. Priority still matters.", example: "Respond to an opposing spell with Discipline; your newer spell resolves first after both players pass." },
  { term: "Hidden", explanation: "Lets an eligible card be hidden for one power of any domain at a battlefield you control. On a later turn it can be played with Reaction timing while ignoring its base cost, subject to its restrictions. Losing control sends the facedown card to the trash at cleanup.", example: "Hide Hidden Blade now, then target a friendly or enemy unit at that same battlefield on a subsequent turn." },
  { term: "Assault", explanation: "Adds the stated amount of Might while the unit is an attacker.", example: "Daring Poro's printed 2 Might becomes 3 while attacking; it does not get the bonus while defending." },
  { term: "Tank", explanation: "Requires combat damage to be assigned to the unit before units without that protection, following the damage-assignment rules.", example: "If Sett needs 5 damage to die and the enemy only has 4, all 4 must go to Sett before another unprotected friendly unit." },
  { term: "Deathknell", explanation: "Marks an ability that triggers when the unit dies. The ability still needs to be handled even though the unit left the board.", example: "Watchful Sentry's controller draws a card after it dies." },
  { term: "Accelerate", explanation: "On a card with Accelerate, offers an additional cost as you play that unit so it enters ready instead of exhausted. Units without this keyword do not gain this option.", example: "Pay Accelerate’s additional cost as part of playing the unit; it is not a separate later way to ready it." },
  { term: "Ganking", explanation: "Adds battlefield-to-battlefield destinations to a unit's standard move. It does not remove the move's exhaustion cost or timing restrictions.", example: "A ready unit with Ganking can move from one battlefield to another during a permitted Main-phase window." },
  { term: "Energy", explanation: "The resource used for numeric costs. Basic runes can be exhausted to make one energy each.", example: "Watchful Sentry costs 2 energy, so exhaust two ready runes before playing it." },
  { term: "Power", explanation: "A resource used to pay domain symbols, separate from energy. It is not Might, a unit’s fighting strength. Recycling a basic rune makes power matching its domain.", example: "Ahri needs Mind power. Recycle a Mind rune to pay that symbol; extra energy or Calm power cannot replace it." },
  { term: "Exhaust / ready", explanation: "An exhausted object is turned sideways, often called tapped. Ready it when a rule or effect says to; ordinary units and runes do not ready on demand.", example: "A newly played unit normally waits until your next Awaken phase before it can make a standard move." },
  { term: "Recycle", explanation: "Returns a card to the bottom of its deck. A rune returns to the rune deck. Its recycle ability makes matching power, even when the rune is already sideways; it does not make energy by itself.", example: "Exhaust a basic rune for energy, then select it and choose Recycle for power. The energy stays available, but that rune cannot ready next turn unless it has been channelled back onto the table." },
  { term: "Mulligan", explanation: "An optional opening-hand replacement, once before play. Set aside zero, one or two of your four cards, draw the same number, then put the originals on the bottom of your main deck. You still have four cards and pay no energy or power.", example: "A first-turn hand with an expensive Ahri may be improved by replacing that copy. Your chosen champion stays in its separate zone. If you replace two cards together, randomise their order on the bottom; do not shuffle the whole deck." },
  { term: "Hand", explanation: "The cards you hold and can choose to play when their timing and costs allow. Their faces are private, but the number of cards is public.", example: "Drawing takes the top card of your main deck into your hand. You do not play it just by drawing it." },
  { term: "Base", explanation: "Your own area for units and gear on the table. A unit here can prepare to move to a battlefield, but does not score a battlefield from base.", example: "Play a Sentry into your base. After it readies, move it to a battlefield to contest that battlefield." },
  { term: "Conquer", explanation: "Score by gaining control of a battlefield after the contest is settled, provided you have not already scored that battlefield this turn.", example: "Finishing combat with your unit as the only survivor can let you conquer." },
  { term: "Hold", explanation: "Score a battlefield you continue to control during your Beginning phase, subject to the once-per-turn scoring rule.", example: "Protect your unit on a battlefield through the opponent's turn so you can hold it in your next Beginning phase." },
  { term: "Priority", explanation: "Your current opportunity to act or pass. You still need a legal action, the right timing and its cost; having priority does not let you play every card.", example: "Pass priority to let your opponent respond; only after all players pass does the next waiting item resolve." },
  { term: "Chain", explanation: "The ordered group of spells and abilities waiting to happen. Resolving means carrying out an effect. The newest waiting item resolves first after the required passes; announcing it does not make it happen immediately.", example: "Two Disciplines resolve separately, newest first, with the appropriate response opportunities." },
  { term: "Counter", explanation: "Remove a waiting spell from the chain without its effect happening. Paid costs stay spent. Follow the counterspell’s target restrictions.", example: "Defy can stop Rune Prison before it stuns a unit. Defy itself can be responded to before resolving." },
  { term: "Might", explanation: "A unit's current combat strength. Effects can make it differ from the printed number.", example: "A temporary +2 Might can let a unit survive damage that would have killed it before the spell resolved." },
  { term: "Buff", explanation: "A +1 Might buff is a specific game status. It is different from an effect simply giving a unit temporary Might.", example: "Discipline's +2 this turn is not automatically a buff to spend for Monastery of Hirana." },
  { term: "Stun", explanation: "A stunned unit contributes no Might to combat damage this turn. Stun does not reduce its Might, exhaust it or stop a move by itself.", example: "A stunned 4-Might defender deals no combat damage, but still needs 4 damage to die. Stun ends in end-of-turn cleanup." },
  { term: "Recall", explanation: "Relocates a permanent to its base. Recall is not a move and does not trigger abilities that look for moving.", example: "Recall an exhausted unit to base and it stays exhausted. Damage and statuses remain unless another rule or effect changes them." },
  { term: "Banish", explanation: "Puts a card into Banishment, a separate place from the trash. Banishing is different from killing or discarding.", example: "A unit does not trigger Deathknell merely because it was banished. Follow any text that specifically refers to Banishment." },
  { term: "Burn", explanation: "Puts the stated number of cards from the top of a player’s main deck into their trash. This is not damage.", example: "Burn 2 sends the top two main-deck cards to the trash. It does not remove victory points or deal damage to a unit." },
  { term: "Predict", explanation: "Privately inspect the stated number of top main-deck cards, recycle any of them, then return the rest to the top in any order. Without a number, inspect one.", example: "With Predict 2, you can keep both on top in your chosen order, recycle one or recycle both. You do not draw them or reveal them to your opponent." }
];

